## Common Mistakes

1. **Not tracking dependencies** — Using npm install without lockfiles, or not maintaining a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM). You can't fix what you don't know you're running.

2. **Ignoring transitive dependencies** — Auditing direct dependencies but not their dependencies. Most supply chain attacks target deeply nested packages.

3. **Delayed patching** — Knowing about vulnerabilities but not patching because "it works." The average time from CVE publication to active exploitation is shrinking to days.

4. **No automated scanning** — Relying on manual checks instead of automated tools (Dependabot, Snyk, npm audit) in CI/CD pipelines that block builds with known vulnerabilities.

## Secure Implementation

### Tier 1: Critical Fix (5 minutes)

**NEVER use ObjectInputStream - use JSON instead**

```java
// SECURE: JSON serialization using Jackson (without enableDefaultTyping)
package com.example.user.secure;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.RedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;

@Service
public class SecureUserCacheService {

    private final RedisTemplate<String, String> redisTemplate;
    private final ObjectMapper objectMapper;

    public SecureUserCacheService(RedisTemplate<String, String> redisTemplate,
                                 ObjectMapper objectMapper) {
        this.redisTemplate = redisTemplate;
        this.objectMapper = objectMapper;
    }

    public void cacheUser(User user, String userId) throws Exception {
        try {
            // ✅ SECURE: Serialize to JSON (not Java serialization)
            String json = objectMapper.writeValueAsString(user);
            redisTemplate.opsForValue().set("user:" + userId, json);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Cache error", e);
        }
    }

    public User getCachedUser(String userId) throws Exception {
        try {
            // ✅ SECURE: Deserialize from JSON
            String json = redisTemplate.opsForValue().get("user:" + userId);
            if (json == null) return null;

            // JSON parsing cannot execute code - no gadget chains possible
            return objectMapper.readValue(json, User.class);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("JSON parsing error", e);
        }
    }
}

// SECURE: Jackson configuration WITHOUT enableDefaultTyping
@Configuration
public class SecureJacksonConfig {
    @Bean
    public ObjectMapper objectMapper() {
        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();

        // ✅ SECURE: Never call enableDefaultTyping()
        // Default: no polymorphic deserialization
        // JSON only deserializes defined types

        return mapper;
    }
}
```

### Tier 2: Essential Defenses (15 minutes)

**Add ObjectInputFilter if Java Serialization Required**

```java
// SECURE: ObjectInputFilter for Java 9+ deserialization restriction
package com.example.user.secure;

import java.io.*;
import java.util.Set;

public class WhitelistingObjectInputStream extends ObjectInputStream {

    // ✅ Whitelist of safe classes
    private static final Set<String> ALLOWED_CLASSES = Set.of(
        "com.example.user.User",
        "com.example.order.Order",
        "java.lang.String",
        "java.lang.Integer",
        "java.util.ArrayList",
        "java.util.HashMap"
    );

    public WhitelistingObjectInputStream(InputStream in) throws IOException {
        super(in);
    }

    @Override
    protected Class<?> resolveClass(ObjectStreamClass desc) 
            throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {

        String className = desc.getName();

        // ✅ SECURE: Only deserialize whitelisted classes
        if (!ALLOWED_CLASSES.contains(className)) {
            throw new InvalidClassException(
                "Deserialization of " + className + " is not allowed");
        }

        return super.resolveClass(desc);
    }
}

// SECURE: Java 9+ ObjectInputFilter approach
package com.example.security;

import java.io.ObjectInputFilter;

public class DeserializationSecurityFilter implements ObjectInputFilter {

    private static final long MAX_ARRAY_SIZE = 10000;
    private static final long MAX_DEPTH = 50;
    private static final long MAX_REFERENCES = 10000;

    // Block known gadget chain classes
    private static final Set<String> GADGET_CLASSES = Set.of(
        "org.apache.commons.collections.functors.InvokerTransformer",
        "org.apache.commons.collections.functors.ChainedTransformer",
        "org.apache.commons.collections4.functors.InvokerTransformer",
        "org.codehaus.groovy.runtime.ConvertedClosure",
        "org.springframework.beans.factory.ObjectFactory",
        "com.sun.org.apache.xalan.internal.xsltc.trax.TemplatesImpl"
    );

    @Override
    public Status checkInput(FilterInfo info) {
        // ✅ Block gadget chain classes
        if (info.serialClass() != null) {
            String className = info.serialClass().getName();

            if (GADGET_CLASSES.stream().anyMatch(className::contains)) {
                return Status.REJECTED;
            }
        }

        // ✅ Enforce resource limits
        if (info.arrayLength() > MAX_ARRAY_SIZE ||
            info.depth() > MAX_DEPTH ||
            info.references() > MAX_REFERENCES) {
            return Status.REJECTED;
        }

        return Status.UNDECIDED;
    }
}

// SECURE: Remove gadget chain libraries from classpath
/*
Dependencies to REMOVE or RESTRICT:
- org.apache.commons:commons-collections3 (has known gadget chains)
- org.apache.commons:commons-collections4 (if not needed)
- org.codehaus.groovy:groovy (if not needed)
- com.sun.org.apache.xalan:xalan (rarely needed)

Dependencies to PREFER:
- Jackson (JSON parsing, not Java serialization)
- Kryo (modern safe serialization library)
- Protocol Buffers (schema-based serialization)
*/
